1
00:00:01,046 --> 00:00:02,602
This is Control Center.

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:06,619
It takes a business requirement all the way to a governed release.

3
00:00:08,145 --> 00:00:09,132
Here is the problem.

4
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:14,948
A developer copies a ticket and pastes it into the AI assistant in their editor.

5
00:00:14,983 --> 00:00:17,746
Three developers do it three different ways.

6
00:00:17,862 --> 00:00:19,279
One pastes the description.

7
00:00:19,395 --> 00:00:21,845
One pastes half and writes their own prompt.

8
00:00:21,903 --> 00:00:23,540
One pastes just the title.

9
00:00:23,586 --> 00:00:27,092
Same requirement, three different results, and nothing checks the result.

10
00:00:28,585 --> 00:00:32,555
So we stop pasting context, and publish it instead.

11
00:00:32,590 --> 00:00:44,468
Control Center builds one context kit from the approved plan: instructions for the assistant, the stories with their acceptance criteria, the architecture, and a failing test for every criterion.

12
00:00:44,537 --> 00:00:50,296
The kit goes into git, next to the code, and every developer's assistant reads the same files.

13
00:00:51,776 --> 00:00:53,471
The work has two halves.

14
00:00:53,553 --> 00:00:57,570
Upstream, the business and AI agree what done means.

15
00:00:57,628 --> 00:01:01,680
Downstream, developers and AI build until it is proven.

16
00:01:01,715 --> 00:01:06,347
The kit joins the two halves, and at five gates, a named person has to sign.

17
00:01:07,845 --> 00:01:12,617
Upstream, the business uploads the requirement, and AI extracts it.

18
00:01:12,686 --> 00:01:17,307
AI breaks it into stories, each with numbered acceptance criteria.

19
00:01:17,365 --> 00:01:21,754
A named owner checks every item and signs, and the plan locks.

20
00:01:21,788 --> 00:01:24,865
Then the kit is published to git for every team.

21
00:01:26,425 --> 00:01:30,442
In the editor, the developer says: start working on story one.

22
00:01:30,465 --> 00:01:35,771
The assistant reads the kit, finds three criteria, and each one already has a failing test.

23
00:01:35,794 --> 00:01:40,032
The developer asks for a plan, edits it, and says: build the plan.

24
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:42,899
The code is written, and the first test turns green.

25
00:01:46,225 --> 00:01:47,734
Every step is governed.

26
00:01:47,908 --> 00:01:51,124
Five gates, each signed by a named person.

27
00:01:51,217 --> 00:01:55,942
Twelve quality checks, computed from real evidence and never asked of a model.

28
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,309
Nine conditions must pass before quality signs off.

29
00:01:59,367 --> 00:02:01,051
Nothing approves its own work.

30
00:02:02,535 --> 00:02:05,588
If something changes late, it cannot slip through.

31
00:02:05,704 --> 00:02:14,284
Everything built on the change goes stale, and a versioned playbook re-opens exactly the gates it touches, until every step is cleared again.

32
00:02:15,875 --> 00:02:17,907
And Control Center gets better.

33
00:02:17,976 --> 00:02:21,575
When a person corrects the AI, the correction is captured.

34
00:02:21,622 --> 00:02:30,527
The admin panel drafts a better prompt, an operator accepts or rejects it, and every change lands as a new version, on the record.

35
00:02:34,175 --> 00:02:35,452
And it runs.

36
00:02:35,475 --> 00:02:42,174
In one overnight rehearsal, a brand new requirement went all the way to a released application.

37
00:02:42,209 --> 00:02:51,451
A real repository, a reviewer that blocked a story until it was fixed, a merged pull request, and 98.5% test coverage.

38
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:56,617
One operator played every role, and every gate still waited for its signature.

39
00:02:58,095 --> 00:02:59,825
Governance is the product.

40
00:02:59,941 --> 00:03:04,887
Not how much the AI writes, but how many places a named person signs.

